  "summary": "Six weeks, five sessions: heavy upper and lower days, then lighter push, pull and legs days. Every muscle is trained twice a week with the same weight on every set. Effort builds from week 1 to 5; week 6 is lighter.",
  "insights": [
    { "title": "Goal", "body": "Build muscle and strength on five days a week. The first two days focus on heavier compound lifts; the last three add more volume with lighter, stretch-focused exercises, so every muscle gets two different kinds of work each week." },
    { "title": "How it works", "body": "Upper and Lower are the heavy days: main lifts first, alone, with 2.5-3 minutes rest. Push (chest, shoulders, triceps), Pull (back, rear delts, biceps) and Legs use machines, cables and dumbbells for 10-20 reps. Paired exercises (same letter) are supersets: first exercise, about 10 seconds, second exercise, then the full rest." },
    { "title": "A days vs B days", "body": "Instead of A/B copies, this template uses different exercises on the heavy days (Upper, Lower: 8-10 reps) and the volume days (Push, Pull, Legs: 10-20 reps). This gives more variety and trains each muscle across a wide rep range every week. Track each day separately." },
    { "title": "How to progress", "body": "The reps shown are your target. When every set reaches it with clean form, add the smallest step next time (about 1-2.5 kg upper body, 2.5-5 kg legs). If you fall 1-2 reps short, keep the weight and beat it next week. Week 1 stop about 3 reps before failure, week 2 about 2, weeks 3-4 about 1-2, week 5 about 1." },
    { "title": "What to expect", "body": "Week 1 should feel easy while you find your weights. Weeks 2-5 should bring more reps or weight on most lifts. A good week is Upper, Lower, rest, Push, Pull, Legs, rest. Week 6 is lighter: same exercises and weights, half the sets (round up), about 3 reps before failure." },
    { "title": "Key tips", "body": "Warm up with 1-2 lighter sets of each main lift. If a machine is taken, swap to a similar exercise from the library and keep the reps. Only the last set of machine, cable or isolation work may go to failure. If a movement hurts, stop it and see a qualified professional." }
  ],
